1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:03,015
Hi there. In this video,

2
00:00:03,015 --> 00:00:05,340
we'll discuss data
visualization and

3
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:08,505
how it helps inform
decisions for your project.

4
00:00:08,505 --> 00:00:11,985
Data visualization is the
graphical representation

5
00:00:11,985 --> 00:00:15,210
of information to
facilitate understanding.

6
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:17,190
That can include graphs,

7
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:20,055
maps, and tables, to name a few.

8
00:00:20,055 --> 00:00:24,030
Project managers use these
visual representations

9
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:25,440
for a few reasons.

10
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:27,570
They're useful for
communicating data

11
00:00:27,570 --> 00:00:29,820
to others because
they help filter

12
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:32,085
information by
focusing the audience

13
00:00:32,085 --> 00:00:35,260
on the most important
data points and insights.

14
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:38,480
Visualizations are also
a more efficient way

15
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:40,835
to summarize information
because they condense

16
00:00:40,835 --> 00:00:42,740
long ideas and facts into

17
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:46,325
a single image or
representation, and crucially,

18
00:00:46,325 --> 00:00:48,830
they help the viewer
make sense of and

19
00:00:48,830 --> 00:00:51,664
remember the information
being presented.

20
00:00:51,664 --> 00:00:54,349
Visualizations help
aid information

21
00:00:54,349 --> 00:00:56,825
processing and enhance memory.

22
00:00:56,825 --> 00:01:00,020
It plays an active role in
the storytelling process by

23
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:03,430
helping communicate project
insights to your audience.

24
00:01:03,430 --> 00:01:05,660
Let's go into some familiar types

25
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:07,220
of data visualizations that

26
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:08,900
project managers use throughout

27
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:11,690
the entire life cycle
of the project.

28
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:16,265
First, let's discuss a common
data visualization tool

29
00:01:16,265 --> 00:01:19,330
used when running the
project: a dashboard.

30
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:22,685
A dashboard is a type
of user interface,

31
00:01:22,685 --> 00:01:26,030
typically a graph or
summary chart, that provides

32
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:27,380
a snapshot view of

33
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:30,175
your project's progress
or performance.

34
00:01:30,175 --> 00:01:32,930
It acts as a centralized
location for

35
00:01:32,930 --> 00:01:36,215
project stakeholders to
draw quick insights.

36
00:01:36,215 --> 00:01:38,780
It can display a tight
summary of metrics,

37
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:43,490
stats, and key performance
indicators, or KPIs.

38
00:01:43,490 --> 00:01:47,825
A KPI is a measurable
value or metric that

39
00:01:47,825 --> 00:01:50,315
demonstrates how
effective an organization

40
00:01:50,315 --> 00:01:53,230
is at achieving key objectives.

41
00:01:53,230 --> 00:01:56,240
They serve as a great aid
in helping your team and

42
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:59,300
stakeholders understand if
you're on the right track.

43
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:01,610
In other words, they
signal if you're

44
00:02:01,610 --> 00:02:04,810
making progress to reach
your success criteria.

45
00:02:04,810 --> 00:02:07,730
In your dashboard, you
may have a summary of

46
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:11,260
your top KPIs or metrics
and your progress to date.

47
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:13,010
For instance, if one of

48
00:02:13,010 --> 00:02:15,395
your objectives is for
your project to reach

49
00:02:15,395 --> 00:02:18,635
a 95% customer
satisfaction score

50
00:02:18,635 --> 00:02:20,765
at the end of a
three-month period,

51
00:02:20,765 --> 00:02:22,700
you may track that goal via

52
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,630
thousands of customer
satisfaction surveys.

53
00:02:25,630 --> 00:02:29,104
Rather than displaying a
spreadsheet of each response,

54
00:02:29,104 --> 00:02:31,010
a dashboard is a good place to

55
00:02:31,010 --> 00:02:33,350
summarize those
results and showcase

56
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:35,840
the average customer
satisfaction score

57
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:38,890
to see how you are
pacing to your goal.

58
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:42,335
Similarly, you could also include

59
00:02:42,335 --> 00:02:45,485
other key KPIs that
signify progress,

60
00:02:45,485 --> 00:02:48,830
like a countdown that shows
the number of days until

61
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:51,110
project launch or the percentage

62
00:02:51,110 --> 00:02:53,410
of the number of issues resolved.

63
00:02:53,410 --> 00:02:55,715
Many project dashboards may

64
00:02:55,715 --> 00:02:58,310
also summarize project
plans, documents,

65
00:02:58,310 --> 00:03:00,290
and reports in one place

66
00:03:00,290 --> 00:03:03,725
and provide a visual of the
status of each of them.

67
00:03:03,725 --> 00:03:06,260
For example, if your
project plan has

68
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:09,364
hundreds of tasks with varying
degrees of completion,

69
00:03:09,364 --> 00:03:11,990
your dashboard may
summarize the amount of

70
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:15,020
tasks or milestones completed
at that point in time

71
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:16,970
and the percentage
of tasks that are in

72
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:19,720
progress, complete, or not started.

73
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:22,055
As you may start to notice,

74
00:03:22,055 --> 00:03:25,070
dashboards are great
visualizations for

75
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:27,140
efficient status updates because

76
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:28,610
they enable you to group,

77
00:03:28,610 --> 00:03:32,555
summarize, and highlight
top project data points.

78
00:03:32,555 --> 00:03:35,420
Another visualization
that does this is

79
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:38,570
one that we mentioned
earlier: a burndown chart.

80
00:03:38,570 --> 00:03:42,170
A burndown chart is a
line chart that measures

81
00:03:42,170 --> 00:03:44,180
the time against
the amount of work

82
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:46,790
done and the amount
of work remaining.

83
00:03:46,790 --> 00:03:49,070
The outstanding
work is usually on

84
00:03:49,070 --> 00:03:53,270
the vertical axis, with the
time visualized horizontally.

85
00:03:53,270 --> 00:03:56,630
This serves as a strong
visualization to help

86
00:03:56,630 --> 00:04:00,960
the team picture the amount
of tasks left to complete.

87
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:03,650
Similar to line charts,

88
00:04:03,650 --> 00:04:06,230
column charts are
another popular graph

89
00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:09,895
used to signal project
performance and progress.

90
00:04:09,895 --> 00:04:13,055
Column charts are
useful for comparing

91
00:04:13,055 --> 00:04:16,540
different activities or
comparing progress over time.

92
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:19,685
For instance, you can
show different outputs,

93
00:04:19,685 --> 00:04:21,080
like the number of customers

94
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:22,685
and the number of
plants delivered,

95
00:04:22,685 --> 00:04:26,290
year-over-year, to demonstrate
growth in change.

96
00:04:26,290 --> 00:04:29,570
Pie charts are useful when
showing the composition of

97
00:04:29,570 --> 00:04:33,025
something or the parts-to-whole
relationship.

98
00:04:33,025 --> 00:04:36,500
All of these simple charts
offer visualizations that

99
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:39,980
enable you to draw quick
insights and help tell stories.

100
00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:41,690
There are many more in

101
00:04:41,690 --> 00:04:42,890
the following reading that I

102
00:04:42,890 --> 00:04:45,560
recommend checking
out and practicing.

103
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:47,600
Before that, there is

104
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:50,195
a final visual tool that
I want you to know about,

105
00:04:50,195 --> 00:04:52,415
which is an infographic.

106
00:04:52,415 --> 00:04:55,310
Infographics are visual
representations of

107
00:04:55,310 --> 00:04:57,785
information, such
as data or facts,

108
00:04:57,785 --> 00:05:00,665
and are typically in the form
of what we call at Google

109
00:05:00,665 --> 00:05:02,960
a "one-pager" or a "one-sheeter."

110
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:04,730
The difference is that they're

111
00:05:04,730 --> 00:05:07,400
typically concise
summaries of that data.

112
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:09,140
This is often done

113
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:11,330
primarily through
graphics or drawings,

114
00:05:11,330 --> 00:05:13,385
emphasizing the
biggest points with

115
00:05:13,385 --> 00:05:15,995
added text for
further explanation.

116
00:05:15,995 --> 00:05:18,110
Use infographics to present

117
00:05:18,110 --> 00:05:20,765
complex information
quickly, professionally,

118
00:05:20,765 --> 00:05:23,240
and clearly, especially
when you may

119
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:26,500
not be present to share
all the details yourself.

120
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:29,240
Infographics should be
able to communicate

121
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:31,265
strong information
without the need for

122
00:05:31,265 --> 00:05:33,610
extra support and explanation.

123
00:05:33,610 --> 00:05:35,765
These are just a few examples of

124
00:05:35,765 --> 00:05:38,090
data visualization in
project management.

125
00:05:38,090 --> 00:05:40,790
Remember, you'll want
to use visuals to

126
00:05:40,790 --> 00:05:42,800
demonstrate and
illustrate situations

127
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:44,315
like changes over time,

128
00:05:44,315 --> 00:05:46,880
frequency, relationship
correlations,

129
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:49,490
and to analyze value and risks.

130
00:05:49,490 --> 00:05:52,280
Another important tip
is to make sure that

131
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:54,875
these visualizations
are accessible.

132
00:05:54,875 --> 00:05:56,405
As we mentioned before,

133
00:05:56,405 --> 00:05:58,355
you'll want to ensure
that your data story

134
00:05:58,355 --> 00:06:00,565
is understood by everyone.

135
00:06:00,565 --> 00:06:02,600
Check out the resources tab

136
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:04,550
on some of the best practices for

137
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:09,075
accessible visualizations and
communications. Nice job!

138
00:06:09,075 --> 00:06:10,880
Now that you're
noticing how to use

139
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:13,130
data to effectively
tell your story,

140
00:06:13,130 --> 00:06:16,160
we'll continue on to the
final piece of this lesson:

141
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:18,200
learning about
presentation techniques

142
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:19,430
to present your data.

143
00:06:19,430 --> 00:06:21,690
Meet you in the next video.
